/*
------------------------------------------------------------
Problem    : Subsets
Platform   : LeetCode
Topic      : Backtracking
Difficulty : Medium

Problem:
Given an array of unique integers, return all possible
subsets of the array.

The result must contain every possible subset exactly once.

The number of subsets of an array of size n is:

    2^n

Example:

    nums = [1,2,3]

Possible subsets:

    []
    [1]
    [2]
    [1,2]
    [3]
    [1,3]
    [2,3]
    [1,2,3]

Approach:
Use Backtracking / Recursion.

For every element, we have exactly two choices:

    1. Do not include the element.
    2. Include the element.

Therefore, every element creates two branches.

Example for:

    nums = [1,2]

Recursion tree:

                    []
                  /    \
               skip    take
                /        \
              []          [1]
             / \          / \
          skip take    skip take
           |     |       |     |
          []    [2]     [1]  [1,2]

Final subsets:

    []
    [2]
    [1]
    [1,2]

Backtracking:
When an element is selected:

    output.push_back(element);

After exploring that branch, remove the element:

    output.pop_back();

This allows us to reuse the same vector for the next
possibility.

Algorithm:
1. Start from index 0.
2. At every index, first choose NOT to include the element.
3. Recursively process the next index.
4. Then include the current element.
5. Recursively process the next index.
6. Remove the element using backtracking.
7. When index reaches nums.size(), store the current subset.

Why does this work?

Every element has exactly two possibilities:

    Include
    Exclude

For n elements, this creates:

    2 × 2 × 2 × ... × 2 = 2^n

possible subsets.

Since the input contains unique elements, every subset
generated by these choices is also unique.

Example:

    nums = [1,2,3]

At index 0:
    Exclude 1
    Include 1

At index 1:
    Exclude 2
    Include 2

At index 2:
    Exclude 3
    Include 3

This generates all 2^3 = 8 subsets.

Time Complexity : O(n * 2^n)

Reason:
There are 2^n subsets, and copying each subset into the
answer can take up to O(n) time.

Space Complexity : O(n)

Reason:
The recursion depth is at most n and the current subset
contains at most n elements.

The output itself requires O(n * 2^n) space.

------------------------------------------------------------
*/

class Solution {
private:

    void backtrack(
        vector<int>& nums,
        int index,
        vector<int>& current,
        vector<vector<int>>& result
    ) {

        // Every subset is valid.
        // Therefore, store the current subset at every step.
        result.push_back(current);

        // No more elements are available.
        if (index == nums.size()) {
            return;
        }

        for (int i = index; i < nums.size(); i++) {

            // Choose the current element
            current.push_back(nums[i]);

            // Explore further elements
            backtrack(nums, i + 1, current, result);

            // Undo the choice
            current.pop_back();
        }
    }

public:

    vector<vector<int>> subsets(vector<int>& nums) {

        vector<vector<int>> result;
        vector<int> current;

        backtrack(nums, 0, current, result);

        return result;
    }
};
